Trans:
How Ukraine and the Allies prepared the counteroffensive and why the original plan was changed. Details.

**According to [NYT data](https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/13/us/politics/ukraine-russia-pentagon.html) and its sources:
On Zelensky’s orders, the Ukrainian military developed a plan for a broad offensive in the south to retake Kherson and cut off Mariupol from Russian troops in the east;
During the “test” of the plan in the game format, it was found that it would fail, and Ukraine would suffer heavy losses. These results were reported to Zelensky, after which the plan of Kiev changed;
Instead of one large offensive, the Ukrainian military proposed two — in Kherson and near Kharkov;
The United States, the United Kingdom and Ukraine assessed the plan and the three parties agreed that the new plan would work.
US officials insist that the credit for the offensive lies entirely with Zelensky and the Ukrainian military, who won a huge victory in Kharkiv with relatively small forces.

Also, it is noted that in Ukraine believe that the APU still need to retake Kherson, liberate the Zaporozhye nuclear power plant and cut off supplies of Russian troops through Mariupol.

